Fix IMAP login failing for accounts whose password contains non-ASCII characters. Such a password cannot go into an IMAP quoted string, so the front's dovecot proxy sends it to the backend as a synchronizing literal and — against RFC 3501 4.3 and RFC 9051 4.3/7.6 — does not wait for the `+ OK` continuation it obliges the backend to send. That continuation is written to the connection's pre-multiplex output stream, so it lands in front of the 9-byte header that opens the multiplex format the two sides have just switched to. The framing never synchronises, `L OK Logged in` never reaches the front, and the login is aborted after 30 seconds with `Login timed out in state=id+capability+login/banner` — even though the backend had long since logged the user in. dovecot 2.4.1 on alpine 3.22 was not affected; 2.4.5 on alpine 3.23 is. The front now authenticates to the backend with `AUTHENTICATE` instead of `LOGIN`, which carries the credentials base64-encoded and inline and never builds a literal. Only IMAP was affected; POP3 and submission were not.
